&nbsp;
Hi Theo 
&nbsp;
Thank you for your time earlier and agreeing to provide comment on this letter. If you could please provide your thoughts on the entirety of the letter it would be greatly appreciated.

&nbsp;
I attach the latest draft of the letter which amends the funding disclosure section to align it more closely with witness statements recently submitted to court.

&nbsp;
We discussed with Simon yesterday some of the assumptions and caveats we are making in our calculations of projected expenditure to July 2026 (when we expect to receive a decision on PDD). It
 would be helpful to confirm your /Simon’s views on us making these statements in the letter:

&nbsp;


Para 9a confirms that costs for the ADR/Settlement phase has not been included in the expenditure calculations as they have not yet been approved

Para 9b confirms that costs for the Expert Reports phase have not been included in the expenditure calculations as they have not yet been approved
Para 13 confirms we are reducing the percentages applied to claimed incurred costs and costs of cost budgeting, the rationale being provided in para 16 (i.e. they are disproportionate and distort the sufficiency
 analysis)
&nbsp;
As discussed, these assumptions around calculating expenditure to the decision on PDD, which we believe are reasonable, help to demonstrate that we currently have sufficient cover. Once these
 points are factored into the calculations it is likely that the shortfall in ATE cover to July 26 will increase.

&nbsp;
Internally, we are working toward increasing our level of cover, however we know that there are issues around capacity in the market and there may be challenges in securing topups to our cover.
 We therefore do not feel that it is appropriate to disclose that we are looking to increase capacity in this letter.

&nbsp;
Please let us know your thoughts

&nbsp;
Regards 
Tom
