 with any relevant updates following the hearings as required.


Below I set out an update in respect of those three substantive hearings and a general update on the Ford Litigation.


De-designation Hearing
(“DDH”)


One key hurdle the parties face in these Pan-NOx Litigations is that there are significant barriers erected between the Litigations that prevent
 the efficient and effective exchange and comparison of information and disclosure between the cases. This is a fundamental part of the Claimants claim i.e. that many of the manufacturers were either sharing strategies and technology to insert prohibits defeat
 devices (“PDDs”) in their vehicles, or alternatively, that better technology was available that was being used by other manufacturers that mean the Defendants did not need to insert PDDs in their vehicles.


As mentioned in previous updates, much the information that exists in the Ford Litigation, and similarly in the other Lead Cases (Mercedes, Renault/Nissan
 and Peugeot Citroën) is subject to a case specific Confidentiality Ring Orders (“CRO”). Each of the Lead cases has their own CRO. The Defendants have taken this CRO as a means to try and keep secret the majority of the documentation that they have provided
 to us, thereby restricting access to the documents to persons who are members of the CRO. Despite protestations from the Defendants, we have successfully implemented a Pan-Lead CRO, which has been constructed to assist in the sharing of confidential information
 across the cases. While this is helpful in permitted us to see documents from other cases, it does not alleviate the issue of transferring documents into the Pan-Lead CRO (as the Defendants continue to raise various issues regarding this) and it does not prevent
 those documents being confidential and only certain people being able to see them. This does not go to the principle of open justice, whereby the public should know the conduct of these manufacturers. It is and always has been our intention to seek to de-designate
 these documents so that they can be referred to openly and in public.


The DDH is shaped at doing precisely that; bringing those documents into the public and also to make the process of hearing these cases in court
 more feasible. Under present circumstances, any time a party wishes to discuss any documentation that is confidential, it would require the Court room to be emptied of people who are not within the CROs, which is simply unworkable. The DDH is intended to allow
 open justice to be carried out.&nbsp;


