nderstand that a litigation funder&nbsp;will have a legitimate interest in reviewing certain documents, and may quite properly wish to have input where those documents either (a) give rise to a financial liability on the funder, or (b) relate to compliance with the funding terms.We also acknowledge PG’s position under the funding arrangements in particular, the covenant requiring PG to obtain the funder’s prior approval before engaging any new law firm or barrister to whom PG grants a contingent interest. This requirement exists because such engagements could create a financial exposure that Gramercy may ultimately need to meet.However, the Pan-NOx Agency Agreement&nbsp;does not&nbsp;fall within that category. It does not create any contingent interests, nor does it involve any cost contribution obligations from the Non-Lead Firms. Its purpose is purely to define the principal–agent relationship&nbsp;between Pogust Goodhead (as Lead Solicitor) and the Non-Lead Firms, governing the conduct, management, strategy decisions and coordination of the litigation. In short, it enables PG to act independently as Lead Solicitor within the proceedings.Similarly, the Litigation Management Agreement is not a funding or liability document. Rather, it is an agreement between the Client Committee and Pogust Goodhead, setting out the instructions, authority and governance framework&nbsp;under which PG acts for the claimants within each GLO. It defines procedural and operational responsibilities, not financial exposure, and is an essential component of PG’s authority to manage the litigation.The level of control that Gramercy has sought to exercise over these documents &nbsp;which govern solicitor-client relationships, not funder obligations&nbsp; goes far beyond the usual parameters of a lender–borrower relationship. It represents an overreach into the conduct and management of the litigation itself, rather than oversight of financial risk. That distinction is fundamental, and Gramercy’s continued involvement in these matters strays beyond what is permissible for a funder maintaining a proper arm’s-length relationship with the litigation.It therefore&nbsp;appears that our collective position is that PG is&nbsp;now openly operating in a world where the funder of the firm has direct management/control of aspects of the litigation - and that control is on-going, despite representations&nbsp;to the contrary made to the Court, the Defendants and to the staff. This creates significant issues for the staff working on these Litigations both in our duties to our clients and to the clients of the other firms in our firm's role as Lead Solicitors.&nbsp;&nbsp;Can you please advise on the following:&nbsp;why representations have been made to the Court, Defendants and staff that Gramercy do not have any role in the conduct, control or management of the NOx Group Litigations when plainly - they do and continue to do so;whether the Board intends to update the Court as to the true position; andwhether the Board intends to inform the clients and other claimant firms of Gramercy's input into documents that have a direct bearing on costs and client instructions.&nbsp;We are continuing to consider this on-going issue as more information comes to light. All of our rights are reserved.&nbsp;&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael, Priyanka, Sarah&nbsp;​​​​Michael&nbsp;Burke(He/Him)Partner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7960&nbsp;032&nbsp;34407960&nbsp;032&nbsp;344&nbsp;"}

