practice in New York and their professional conduct is regulated by the New York Rules of Professional Conduct.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;This email (and any attachments) is confidential and may also be legally privileged and is intended solely for the use of the individual or entity addressed above. If you are not the intended recipient, please immediately notify the sender, then delete it from your system. You should not copy it or use it for any purpose, nor disclose its contents to any other person. Please also note that emails sent or received may be monitored to ensure compliance with the law and with our policies.&nbsp;Further regulatory information can be found in the Legal Notices which appear on our website at&nbsp;https://www.pallasllp.com/legal.​​​​From: Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 26 September 2025 17:09To: Plate Litigation &lt;plate_litigation@pallasllp.com&gt;Cc: Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Craig Shuttleworth &lt;cshuttleworth@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Fw: CFO01: Ford Client Committee - Strictly Private and Confidential&nbsp;All&nbsp;This went without me having sight of it or considering first.&nbsp;&nbsp;Anna&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Anna&nbsp;VargaPartner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;699This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our&nbsp;website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Friday, September 26, 2025 4:52 PMTo:&nbsp;Richard Hayes &lt;rhayes47@btinternet.com&gt;; 'tobyh45@gmail.com' &lt;tobyh45@gmail.com&gt;; dinahlogan@hotmail.com &lt;dinahlogan@hotmail.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Ford Litigation Team &lt;fordteam@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: CFO01: Ford Client Committee - Strictly Private and Confidential &nbsp;STRICTLY PRIVATE AND CONFIDENTIALDear all,I hope you are all well. I write further to my last update on 5 August 2025, to provide you with a brief update on the following matters as they relate to the Ford NOx Emissions Litigation (the “Ford Litigation”), and the Pan-NOx Litigation generally (the “Litigation”):·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; PDD Trial Preparation;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Claimants Mechanical Expert;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Quantum Trial and discontinuance;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Change in personnel.PDD Trial Preparation and final Pre-Trial ReviewWe are fast approaching the commencement of the PDD Trial, with the first day of trial listed for 13 October 2025, and the trial due to run until 19 December 2025.Our skeleton argument was filed and served on 19 September, representing a mammoth undertaking by the entire Solicitor team and the barristers. The Ford specific annex to the skeleton argument was filed and served on 24 September. There was a slight extension to the Ford annex on account of the Defendants providing a very late second witness statement from a Ford employee. While we were not obliged to permit this into evidence, we took the view after considering it, that the statement was in fact more helpful for us and the case to allow its entry, rather than refusing permissions. As a caveat to our permittance of its inclusion, we agreed a short extension to the Ford annex.As soon as I am in a position to provide you with a copy of the skeleton argument (with redactions for confidential information) I will send this to you. I would hope this will be later this week. I am extremely pleased with the final version of the skeleton argument. Our counsel team have done a remarkable job and it presents the best possible case for the court to consider.While negotiations on the final timetable continue between the parties, we are close to concluding the timetable.The first week of trial, due to commence on 13 October 2025, will consist of the Claimants and the Defendants making their opening remarks to the Court, in essence setting the scene, before we then move into evidence on what constitutes Normal Driving Conditions and then turning to each individual manufacturer. The order of the manufacturers will be Mercedes, Ford, Peugeot Citroen, Renault and Nissan.It is expected that the Ford evidence, including the cross examination of experts and witnesses, will start on or around 13 November and run for just over a week.If any of you are interested in attending Court for any part of the trial, please let us know and we will be able to arrange this with you.There is due to be a final “Pre-Trial Review” hearing before the Court on 6 October which we are preparing for and this hearing will aim to settle any final issues or disputes that may impact the smooth running of the trial. The parties are in the process of agreeing an agenda for this hearing, and we will provide you with a further update on this in due course.Claimants’ Software and Mechanical ExpertsAs per my previous update, I confirmed that in a hearing on 10 September, the Court unfortunately did not side with the Claimants in respect of the expert applications, and gave some strict directions with regard to steps the parties were required to take in respect of the software evidence that had been prepared.We have been working hard to prepare and finalise the software report with the redactions / removals as advised by the Court and a version of these amended reports was provided to the Defendants on 24 September. We are awaiting the Defendants’ response to this draft report. &nbsp;In respect of the Claimants' mechanical expert, the Claimants' barrister team have provided a note to us in which they have flagged certain concerns with the mechanical expert. They have raised that they are concerned how the expert will perform at trial and that certain precautions need to be taken with the expert. Importantly, however, they have not told us that these concerns impact on the prospects of success of the case and we remain confident in the case we have prepared against Ford.We are taking this note incredibly seriously and we are working hard, with the other Lead Solicitors, to prepare the expert for the trial. We are confident that, even with these concerns, the expert will be suitably prepared for trial, having undergone significant preparatory work before he takes the stand.The expert is due to travel to the UK, from America, on 29 September, and will largely be based here, with the team, until his evidence is complete. He will observe various cross-examinations of other experts and will get a good feel for the experience he is likely to have in the witness box.The team will also be assisting him in his preparation.If you have any questions regarding this, please do let me and the Ford team know.Quantum TrialAs you are aware, the Court has ordered that a second trial be listed in these NOx Litigations for October 2026. This trial, referred to as the “Quantum Trial” is in relation to the value of certain claims that are being brought by the Claimants. The Court will assess what value should be attributed to different heads of loss claimed by the Claimants, each of which were sustained as a result of the Defendants’ conduct, and determine a value for each claim being brought.The Quantum Trial is proceeding, at least initially, on the basis of assumptions that the vehicles had prohibited defeat devices in them. This is necessary because we will not have a judgment from the Court on the defeat devices until at some point in mid-2026 (post the October 2025 trial), and it is impractical for the entire litigation to grind to a half while waiting for this judgment. As such, we will prepare evidence and submissions on what we have pleaded for the PDD Trial, and this will then be updated, in very short order, once we have that judgment.It is obviously impractical for the Court to hear evidence and consider losses on an individual basis (to do so would defeat the purpose of a group litigation), and so how this trial will proceed is by way of Sample Claimants. These are Claimants, selected between the Claimants and Defendants, who represent a range of characteristics and different issues across the Claimant cohort such that findings by the Court can be applied to all claimants. The characteristics that the Claimants needed to represent were included in Annex 2 to the October 2024 CMC Order, a copy of which is attached again for ease.The process of negotiating these Sample Claimants took some time but the parties have finally reach agreement. A total of 40 Sample Claimants have been negotiated between the Claimants and Defendants across the Pan-NOx Litigations, split as eight per manufacturer, and of those eight, four selected by the Claimants, and four by the Defendants. Details of the Ford Sample Claimants can be provided if required.Over the course of the negotiations, the Defendants made various nominations that the Claimants wished to resist, for various reasons i.e. the Claimants did not wish to participate as Sample Claimants, or they wished to pursue their claim in a different jurisdiction. In circumstances where those Sample Claimant nominees did not wish to proceed, the Defendants have taken the position that they must have their claims removed from the English Litigation. We have some sympathy with this, especially where Claimants wish to pursue their claim in a different jurisdiction.Kenneth Gormley is one such person. Mr Gormley is a Ford Claimant, was nominated by the Defendants as a Sample Claimant, however we have since identified he is bringing a claim in both the England &amp; Wales jurisdiction as part of this claim, and also in a separate litigation in Scotland.Mr Gormley cannot continue with both claims and he was required to pick which one he wanted to continue with and we have instructions from Mr Gormley that he wishes to pursue his claim as part of the Scottish proceedings. We therefore are required to prepare and serve a formal notice of discontinuance in respect of Mr Gormley’s claim in the English proceedings.Pursuant to paragraphs 29 and 30 of the Ford Group Litigation Order, we are seeking your instructions to act on Mr Gormley’s instructions and discontinue his claim.Before confirming these instructions, we must advise you of the effect of this discontinuance. While it will not adversely impact the litigation, indeed the Litigation is already proceeding past this, the discontinuance will necessarily result in the Defendants being entitled to incur costs in the Litigation, that will be recoverable by them in due course.As an initial reassurance, these costs will not be due from you or from any of the Claimants, but they will instead be covered, should they need to be paid, by either the ATE provider who is providing cover for this Litigation, or by Mr Gormley himself.The general rule, under the governing Civil Procedure Rules 38.6 is that where a Claimant discontinues all or part of their claim, the Defendant has an automatic right to the costs they have incurred in respect of their claim.There will be two types of costs that the Defendants will be able to recover: common costs, and individual costs.As explained in the Client Committee meeting post the first Cost Management Hearing, the individual costs will be costs solely in relation to Mr Gormley’s claim, and the common costs will be the apportioned share of the Defendants overall costs of this exercise in relation to Mr Gormley’s claim. Mr Gormley’s claim is one claim out of hundreds of thousands, and so while we remain unaware of the level of costs that have been incurred by the Defendants, we believe the overall amount of the common costs will be minimal.These costs cannot be resolved until the conclusion of the case and so there is no immediate cost that will be required to be paid.I am of course available to discuss any aspect of this with you before you confirm your instructions, but I wish to reassure you that this discontinuance will not result in you, or any of the other thousands of claimants in the Litigation, being out of pocket.As a result, we respectfully seek your instructions to:1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Proceed to discontinue Mr Gormley’s claim in England &amp; Wales; and2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Remove Mr Gormley from the Ford Group Register.Please confirm by return that you are content for us to take these stepsChange of PersonnelAs a final piece of information, it is with regret that I must inform you that I have made the difficult decision to resign from my role at Pogust Goodhead.I am currently working my notice period, with my final day of employment with the firm still subject to negotiation. As soon as I know when my final day is, I will inform you of this. I appreciate you will need to know this information.Please rest assured that even with me having made the decision to part from the firm, I will be, and I am, continuing to act in the best interests of the Claimant Committee, and the wider claimant cohort. I personally have devoted a significant amount of time to this case and I am determined to ensure that the case is as best presented at trial as it can be.I must also, at this point, advise you that the Partner with overall conduct of the Pan-NOx Litigations for Pogust Goodhead, Peter Gallagher, has also resigned. I am unable to comment on the reasons for his departure, but he has formally left the firm and the firm is putting in place a new head of the NOx department. They will contact you to confirm who this person is in due course.&nbsp;The remainder of the Ford Pogust Goodhead team remains in situ and they will continue to liaise with you. I remain with conduct of the matter, and when a decision has been made as to who will take conduct of the matter once I have departed, I will arrange for a handover to take place such that you are introduced to the new case lead.Should you have any questions regarding the contents of this letter, please do not hesitate to contact us.As always, we are grateful for your continued support and attention to the matter.Yours faithfully,Michael &amp; the Ford Litigation Team&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Michael&nbsp;Burke(He/Him)Partner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7960&nbsp;032&nbsp;34407960&nbsp;032&nbsp;344From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Monday, September 08, 2025 5:05 PMTo:&nbsp;Richard Hayes &lt;rhayes47@btinternet.com&gt;; 'tobyh45@gmail.com' &lt;tobyh45@gmail.com&gt;; dinahlogan@hotmail.com &lt;dinahlogan@hotmail.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Ford Litigation Team &lt;fordteam@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: CFO01: Ford Client Committee - Strictly Private and Confidential&nbsp;STRICTLY PRIVATE AND CONFIDENTIALDear all,I hope you are all well. I write further to my last update on 5 August 2025, to provide you with a brief update on the Ford NOx Emissions Litigation (the “Ford Litigation”), and the Pan-NOx Litigation generally (the “Litigation”)As identified in my previous update, the Defendants have made a series of complaints about the expert evidence that we have submitted in the Ford Litigation, most specifically the expert evidence of our automotive software engineer, Dr Marcus Heitz. Each of the other Lead Defendants have made similar applications to restrict or reduce the evidence of Dr Heitz.&nbsp;&nbsp;In parallel to this, Ford have also sought to disapply generic expert evidence from the expert in the Mercedes case and also Ford related material in the expert who provided a report on Normal Driving Conditions, Dr Tate.&nbsp;&nbsp;The Defendants set out their substantive complaints with Dr Heitz's report on 8 August, highlighting almost 50% of his report as being subject to complaint and seeking its removal. Despite attempting to reasonably engage with them, including sending them a table of potential points we thought capable of agreement, Ford launched an application on 27 August 2025 seeking the entirety of their proposed removals be retained. This is also the case for the generic material in Mercedes and Dr Tate's evidence.&nbsp;&nbsp;The Claimants have finalised and served their skeleton argument today and we are in the process of exchanging these with the Defendants.&nbsp;&nbsp;The hearing is listed for Wednesday, 10 September 2025, and I will update you on the outcome of the hearing later in the week.&nbsp;&nbsp;If you have any questions, please do not hesitate to contact me to discuss.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael &amp; the Ford Litigation Team&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Michael&nbsp;Burke(He/Him)Partner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7960&nbsp;032&nbsp;34407960&nbsp;032&nbsp;344From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Tuesday, August 05, 2025 10:57 AMTo:&nbsp;Richard Hayes &lt;rhayes47@btinternet.com&gt;; 'tobyh45@gmail.com' &lt;tobyh45@gmail.com&gt;; dinahlogan@hotmail.com &lt;dinahlogan@hotmail.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Ford Litigation Team &lt;fordteam@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: CFO01: Ford Client Committee - Strictly Private and Confidential&nbsp;STRICTLY PRIVATE AND CONFIDENTIALDear all,I hope you are all well. I write further to my last update on 29 June 2025, to provide you with an update on the Ford NOx Emissions Litigation (the “Ford Litigation”), and the Pan-NOx Litigation generally (the “Litigation”)As always, please note that the contents and existence of this letter are strictly privileged and confidential&nbsp;and should not be discussed with anyone other than us, as your legal representative, or other members of the Ford Claimant Committee.&nbsp;In my previous update, I confirmed that we were set for an exceptionally busy July, with three hearings listed (De-designation, Cost Management and a Pre-Trial Review), in addition to the exchange of expert reports. I am pleased to say that we have successfully navigated each of those hearings, and exchanged our expert reports, and I set out below a full update in respect of each of those items.&nbsp;&nbsp;De-designation Hearing (“DDH”)&nbsp;This DDH took place from 7 - 9 July before Mr Justice Constable and I am pleased to announce that it was a success for the Claimants. The judgment following the hearing was released on 25 July 2025 and can be found here: https://www.judiciary.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Various-Claimants-v-Mercedes-Benz-Ford-Nissan-Stellantis-and-others.pdf&nbsp;You can also find a helpful summary of the judgment here along with commentary from Pogust Goodhead and Leigh Day: https://pogustgoodhead.com/dieselgate-high-court-blocks-attempts-by-car-manufacturers-to-hide-key-documents-that-could-show-whether-they-cheated-emissions-tests/&nbsp;In short, the Court ruled that the Manufacturer Defendants cannot hide behind the cloak of confidentiality and must reveal hundreds of documents, including the parameters that the vehicles operate within, to the public. The Claimants were entirely justified in their application to lift confidentiality from these documents and the hearing will now be conducted in open court and subject to little or no reporting restrictions. There are still some documents that will retain their confidentiality, but it is a small number in comparison to the overall number of documents in the litigations.&nbsp;&nbsp;This is a significant victory in these cases, including in the Ford case, as it now means the Defendants must now reveal to the world how they calibrate and operate their vehicles. It also means that justice will be done publicly and without restriction - something the Defendants fought very hard to maintain. It further increases scrutiny on the Defendants from regulators and other public bodies to ensure that they are more closely examined and held to standard.&nbsp;We are presently working on assembling all relevant documents that have previously been provided to you in redacted form, such as the Individual Particulars of Claim and we will update the SharePoint accordingly with those unredacted documents.&nbsp;&nbsp;Cost Management Hearing (“CMH”)&nbsp;The CMH took place on 15 and 16 July before Mrs Justice Cockerill. The judgment following this hearing has not yet been handed down but we are expecting it to be over the course of the summer break and hopefully before September. We will update you as soon as we know more regarding this.&nbsp;Pre-Trial Review&nbsp;(“PTR”)&nbsp;The PTR took place on 28 and 29 July, again before Mrs Justice Cockerill. The Court gave guidance on a number of procedural issues including the expected timetable for Trial i.e. what order the cases will be heard in and how long each case will be heard in court, the Trial bundle of documents and protocols governing how the parties are to interact in advance of the hearing. None of this involves any work that you as the Claimants need to undertake.&nbsp;&nbsp;What is of significance however are the two applications that we made to be heard at the PTR in respect of Ford. As forecast in my previous update, we did ultimately make an application for Specific Disclosure of certain additional relevant information from Ford. Following sustained negotiations with the Defendants in respect of this, the parties have been able to reach an agreement on certain specific further documentation that Ford are to provide us with by 15 August 2025.&nbsp;&nbsp;Secondly, and importantly, we submitted an application for permission to amend our pleadings. The need for this arose following our review of documents that we had received from the Defendants which indicated that there is a defect in certain of the Defendant Vehicles which in turn affects the PDDs that we allege are in the vehicles. Ford are nervous about this information and they sought to oppose this amendment to the pleadings. The Court opted to not deal with the application at the PTR but instead to deal with it at a hearing in future, potentially in September.&nbsp;This amendment and bringing the defect to light and into the public will have a significantly positive impact on the case as it bring a host of new pleadings against the Defendants.&nbsp;&nbsp;We will keep you updated on the progress of this application.&nbsp;The other key point the Court dealt with was in relation to expert evidence at the PTR. The Defendants, most notably Ford, have been making a series of on-going complaints regarding the contents of our expert reports, stating that they feel the reports go outside the remit of the experts. We of course aver that they do not, instead considering that the Defendants' complaints are instead a cover for their nervousness about their contents. Ford in particular have been raising issues regarding the software report that we have filed, primarily we believe because our expert has done an excellent job and presented a very damaging case to Ford that increases the prospects of success at trial.&nbsp;&nbsp;The Court has advised that a further hearing on 10 September will be listed to hear any arguments over the contents of the expert reports, with the parties to try and resolve the issues prior to the date of the hearing. We will keep you informed as to this.&nbsp;&nbsp;Ford Litigation specific matters&nbsp;Expert Reports&nbsp;Per the below update, the initial expert reports have now been exchanged (on the 25 and 27 July respectively) and we are moving towards the third step previously advised: the exchange of supplemental expert reports. This is due to occur on the 22nd August.&nbsp;&nbsp;Our initial review of the expert reports provided by the Defendants is that they do not contain any surprises or give us real cause for concern. Their software report is considerably less in-depth than ours, to our advantage, while their mechanical engineer also appears to have struggled to formulate a strong case in Ford's favour.&nbsp;&nbsp;Claimant Committee Members&nbsp;We are continuing to engage with Virgin Media over their appointment to the Committee and there is no substantive update on this at this point.&nbsp;&nbsp;Should any of you have any further questions arising out of this update, please do not hesitate to get in contact with me to discuss.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael &amp; the Ford Litigation Team&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Michael&nbsp;Burke(He/Him)Partner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7960&nbsp;032&nbsp;34407960&nbsp;032&nbsp;344&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Sunday, June 29, 2025 1:08 PMTo:&nbsp;Richard Hayes &lt;rhayes47@btinternet.com&gt;; 'tobyh45@gmail.com' &lt;tobyh45@gmail.com&gt;; dinahlogan@hotmail.com &lt;dinahlogan@hotmail.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Ford Litigation Team &lt;fordteam@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: CFO01: Ford Client Committee - Strictly Private and Confidential&nbsp;STRICTLY PRIVATE AND CONFIDENTIALDear all,I hope you are all well. I write further to my last update in May 2025, to provide you with an update on the Ford NOx Emissions Litigation (the “Ford Litigation”), and the Pan-NOx Litigation generally (the “Litigation”)As always, please note that the contents and existence of this letter are strictly privileged and confidential&nbsp;and should not be discussed with anyone other than us, as your legal representative, or other members of the Ford Claimant Committee.Having previously updated you on the March and May CMCs, I am pleased to inform you that we have now agreed the resultant Orders from those hearings and copies of these are attached.As you will discern, those Orders do update previous directions, especially the May Order which now sets out a more detailed process for disclosure in respect of the Quantum Trial in October 2026, &nbsp;Details of these updates were contained in my previous update.As detailed below, the team is currently preparing for a very busy July including three substantive hearings across the month: the de-designation hearing, which is listed between 7 - 9 July 2025; a Cost Management Hearing in respect of the Quantum Trial, which is listed between 14 – 16 July 2025; and finally the Pre-Trial Review (the “PTR”) which is listed on 28 and 29 July.We are also preparing our final expert reports which are due to be exchanged on 16 July 2025, while supplementary reports are to be exchanged on 30 July.In light of the exceptionally busy July, I may be unable to provide you with a substantive update until August, but I will endeavour to provide you with any relevant updates following the hearings as required.Below I set out an update in respect of those three substantive hearings and a general update on the Ford Litigation.De-designation Hearing (“DDH”)One key hurdle the parties face in these Pan-NOx Litigations is that there are significant barriers erected between the Litigations that prevent the efficient and effective exchange and comparison of information and disclosure between the cases. This is a fundamental part of the Claimants claim i.e. that many of the manufacturers were either sharing strategies and technology to insert prohibits defeat devices (“PDDs”) in their vehicles, or alternatively, that better technology was available that was being used by other manufacturers that mean the Defendants did not need to insert PDDs in their vehicles.As mentioned in previous updates, much the information that exists in the Ford Litigation, and similarly in the other Lead Cases (Mercedes, Renault/Nissan and Peugeot Citroën) is subject to a case specific Confidentiality Ring Orders (“CRO”). Each of the Lead cases has their own CRO. The Defendants have taken this CRO as a means to try and keep secret the majority of the documentation that they have provided to us, thereby restricting access to the documents to persons who are members of the CRO. Despite protestations from the Defendants, we have successfully implemented a Pan-Lead CRO, which has been constructed to assist in the sharing of confidential information across the cases. While this is helpful in permitted us to see documents from other cases, it does not alleviate the issue of transferring documents into the Pan-Lead CRO (as the Defendants continue to raise various issues regarding this) and it does not prevent those documents being confidential and only certain people being able to see them. This does not go to the principle of open justice, whereby the public should know the conduct of these manufacturers. It is and always has been our intention to seek to de-designate these documents so that they can be referred to openly and in public.The DDH is shaped at doing precisely that; bringing those documents into the public and also to make the process of hearing these cases in court more feasible. Under present circumstances, any time a party wishes to discuss any documentation that is confidential, it would require the Court room to be emptied of people who are not within the CROs, which is simply unworkable. The DDH is intended to allow open justice to be carried out.&nbsp;The Claimants prepared and filed their respective applications and witness evidence on 13 June 2025 and we took receipt of the Defendants’ responsive evidence late last night. We are considering this with our counsel team in advance of the preparation of skeleton arguments and the hearing itself.Cost Management Hearing (“CMH”)A Cost Management Hearing is scheduled for 14-16 July 2025. The purpose of this hearing is for the parties to agree the costs budgets in relation to the Quantum Trial, which is set to take place in October 2026.You may recall we undertook a similar exercise last July in respect of costs for the PDD Trial, which we are hurtling towards in October of this year.We have instructed the same counsel team as represented us last year which has assisted us in considering the rulings of the Court at that hearing. Our budgets were exchanged with the Defendants on 17 June and we have been considering them since. We are due to provide a response to the Defendants budgets in documents knows as Budget Discussion Report (“BDRs”) on 3 July and we are working with our counsel team to prepare these now. We image the BDRs may assist in bring about more agreement with the Defendants and in advance of skeleton arguments which are due on 10 July 2025.Again, we will keep you updated on this hearing as we progress towards it.Pre-Trial Review&nbsp;(“PTR”)The final hearing in July and before the summer break is the PTR. Generally this hearing is reserved for procedural matters, including timetabling for the main trial, the running order of issues and the trial bundle, as opposed to any more substantive issues between the parties.However, in these cases which are extraordinary, it is likely that this PTR will deal with a larger number of issues including any final applications that the Claimants may wish to make before the Court takes its summer vacation. In the Ford Litigation, we are consider submitting applications for specific documents however we have not yet arrived at the final decision on that point. &nbsp;Preparation for this hearing has already commenced and the teams are identifying issues that they may require to be resolved at that hearing.Again, we will keep you updated on this hearing as we progress towards it.&nbsp;Ford Litigation specific mattersWithout Prejudice Meeting – HIGHLY CONFIDENTIALA preliminary&nbsp;confidential meeting with Ford took place on 12 June 2025 to discuss the merits of the case. As mentioned in our previous update, the aim of this meeting was to inform the Ford Defendants of the strength of our case and seek to narrow the issues between the parties.In attendance at the meeting were our the Claimant and Defendant counsel teams, as well as some of the Solicitors and Ford also sent one of their internal general counsel to attend. During the meeting, the barristers presented a number of key confidential documents to Fords legal team and ventilated a number of high level issues about the case, many of which the Ford became defensive about. While the meeting has not yielded any immediate results, from the Claimants' perspective it was positive and we viewed the meeting as having done what it needed to.We have not yet heard anything further from Ford (which was expected) but we will keep you updated if and when we hear anything further from them.Claimant Committee MembersWith your previous consent, we have officially appointed Keith Turner as a new Claimant Committee Member. We are awaiting a signed copy of the Claimant Committee Agreement from Virgin Media before they are appointed as a member, and once that is concluded, we will confirm the new Claimant Committee and convene a meeting.This will likely take place in August.&nbsp;TestingAs mentioned in our previous update, the parties have been undergoing tests on all 6 Sample Vehicles. These tests are now concluded, and, as expected, the results in many instances have proven to be excellent in assisting the case we are advancing. The results of the testing have been provided to both the Claimant and Defendant experts who are analysing them to incorporate into their reports.Expert ReportsA crucial step in preparing our case for trial. Our experts (in the fields of automotive software and mechanical engineering) are currently preparing reports outlining their analysis of the test results and their findings and opinion regarding PDDs that are contained in Ford vehicles.This is a 3 step process, the first step having already been concluded.1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A meeting(s) between the experts to formulate a Joint Statement which sets out where the experts opinions align. This has been completed and a copy of the report has been added to the SharePoint.2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Exchange of main Expert reports exchanged between the parties . These reports are compiled independent from the other side and represent the individual exerts opinions and findings based upon the test results and disclosure. Our experts are working hard to finalise their reports and we are meeting with them regularly to assist them in any way we can.3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Exchange of Supplementary Expert Reports. Once the reports at step 2 above are exchanged, the experts have time to consider the content of those reports and then update any opinions in their own report based on what the other expert has said. Work on this will commence immediately after exchange of the reports.Disclosure and Witness EvidenceAs foreshadowed in the previous update, all tranches of disclosure have now been received from the Defendants and a mass review exercise is on-going. To date, the Ford Defendants have disclosed approximately 38,000 documents that the combined teams of Pogust Goodhead and Leigh Day are reviewing these to assist in substantiating our case. Some documents include evidence from the Defendants showing they had updates available for the vehicles, as they knew they were problematic, however they deigned not to implement them at the time for internal reasons, and other documents show that Ford opted for different strategies in their vehicles in comparison to others due to cost considerations.All of this information is incredibly helpful both for our counsel team who will be presenting the information in court but also for our team of experts who will be considering disclosure as part of their report.We have also taken receipt of a Witness Statement served by Ford from one of their employees who was involved in the development of the their diesel engines. They have served this in an effort to rebut our case, however the statement is quite helpful in a number of ways, including alluding to documents which may be damaging to Ford’s case. We and our counsel team are continuing to analyse this, and we are engaging with Ford on the document and its contents. It is thought that this individual will attend the trial and he will be cross-examined by our counsel team.Group Register and Notices of ObjectionThe official deadline for service of the sixth iteration of the Group Register was on Friday, 20 June 2025.While the Group Register, per the terms of the GLO, is due to be served every three months, In light, of the other competing deadlines in the upcoming months, we have agreed with the Defendants to an extension of this deadline to 20 September 2025. We are reviewing our Group Register data ahead of the next iteration and will keep you updated.The Defendants have also provided further objections regarding a small number of claims enrolled in the fourth iteration of the Group Register. As they are only able to raise objections in respect of “new” claims enrolled on the Group Register,&nbsp; or existing claims providing new information, this is a task is diminishing as there are limited “new” claims being entered onto the Group Register. In Comparison to previous rounds, the number of objections raised in this time was significantly smaller with the Defendants only objecting to 16 Pogust Goodhead claims, 2 of which are being removed as duplicates. The remaining claims have been either rejected or reserved, subject to our clients’ instructions.I trust this update reassures you that we are continuing to advance at a breakneck pace towards the trial in October 2025 which will provide a conclusive outcome to the liability aspect of this matter.While we are set for an exceptionally busy July, should any of you require anything from me or the Ford team, please let us know.I hope you all enjoy your summer break and we will be in touch in August.Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael &amp; the Ford Litigation Team&nbsp;Michael&nbsp;Burke(He/Him)Partner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7960&nbsp;032&nbsp;34407960&nbsp;032&nbsp;344&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;18 May 2025 9:41 AMTo:&nbsp;Richard Hayes &lt;rhayes47@btinternet.com&gt;; 'tobyh45@gmail.com' &lt;tobyh45@gmail.com&gt;; dinahlogan@hotmail.com &lt;dinahlogan@hotmail.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Ford Litigation Team &lt;fordteam@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: CFO01: Ford Client Committee - Strictly Private and Confidential&nbsp;STRICTLY PRIVATE AND CONFIDENTIAL&nbsp;Dear all,&nbsp;Thank you very much for your prompt responses regarding Keith joining the Claimant Committee. Each of you have approved Keith joining.&nbsp;&nbsp;We will look to take the next steps to add Keith and Virgin Media to the Committee in the coming weeks and we will be in touch. We will also keep you informed as to how the initial WP meeting with Ford goes.&nbsp;&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael &amp; the Ford Litigation Team&nbsp;Michael&nbsp;Burke(He/Him)Partner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7960&nbsp;032&nbsp;34407960&nbsp;032&nbsp;344This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our Privacy Policy.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Saturday, May 17, 2025 8:30 AMTo:&nbsp;Richard Hayes &lt;rhayes47@btinternet.com&gt;; 'tobyh45@gmail.com' &lt;tobyh45@gmail.com&gt;; dinahlogan@hotmail.com &lt;dinahlogan@hotmail.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Ford Litigation Team &lt;fordteam@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: CFO01: Ford Client Committee - Strictly Private and Confidential&nbsp;STRICTLY PRIVATE AND CONFIDENTIAL&nbsp;Dear all,&nbsp;I am writing further to my previous update below, and further to the previous communications regarding the addition of new Claimant Committee members.&nbsp;&nbsp;Following a further review of our client cohort, we propose adding the following claimant to the Committee:&nbsp;Keith Turner&nbsp;Keith has been selected based on his responses during our investigation, where he demonstrated a strong interest in becoming a member and showed commitment and availability. Keith is a retired Chief Constable of Police, having served 32 years as a serving officer, with 10 years as a Chief Officer. Following his retirement, he worked as a Probation Trust Chair for the Home Office and in the Private sector, as well as a non-executive director of a Communications Company. Now completely retired, he has excellent availability to commit to the Claimant Committee and mentioned being well used to reading and responding to emails and documents. In this Litigation, he is claiming for three Ford Kuga vehicles owned between 2017 and 2021. He has read and agreed to the Claimant Committee Agreement, and, pending your approval, he will be officially added as a fourth member to the Claimant Committee.&nbsp;If you are agreeable to Keith being added, we will arrange for both him and Virgin Media, whom you have already approved to join the Committee, to be added in the coming months.&nbsp;&nbsp;We&nbsp;would therefore be grateful if you&nbsp;could please confirm your approval of Keith becoming a member of the Claimant Committee by responding to this email. My colleague Isabel will follow up with you later this week if we are yet to hear from you.&nbsp;&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael &amp; the Ford Litigation Team&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Friday, May 16, 2025 10:40 AMTo:&nbsp;Richard Hayes &lt;rhayes47@btinternet.com&gt;; 'tobyh45@gmail.com' &lt;tobyh45@gmail.com&gt;; dinahlogan@hotmail.com &lt;dinahlogan@hotmail.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Ford Litigation Team &lt;fordteam@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: CFO01: Ford Client Committee - Strictly Private and Confidential&nbsp;STRICTLY PRIVATE AND CONFIDENTIALDear all,I hope you are well. I write further to my last substantive update in February 2025, to advise you on the outcome of the two Case Management Conferences (“CMC”), and to provide you with an update on the Ford NOx Emissions Litigation (the “Ford Litigation”).As always, please note that the contents of this email are privileged and confidential. Please do not share it with anyone. You may of course discuss them with other members of the Claimant Committee if you would wish.We were due to have a call to discuss the outcome of the CMC that took place from 5 – 7 March 2025, however that call did not go ahead as planned. Following this, we rolled almost straight into the preparation for a second CMC that took place from 7 - 9 May 2025.While the March CMC was primarily focused on issues for the PDD Trial in October 2025, the May CMC was aimed at the Quantum Trial which is listed for October 2026. The Court held a CMC on the Quantum Trial, despite it being 18 months away, as the parties (even with the PDD Trial occurring in October 2025) need to be preparing for the steps after the PDD Trial as we cannot wait until it has concluded before progressing the matters. There needs to be simultaneous progress on all fronts to keep the litigation moving forward.&nbsp;Overall, the Claimants are pleased with the outcomes from both of these CMCs and they keep advancing these cases on an aggressive timetable.&nbsp;March CMC - PDD TrialAs advised in my previous updates, there were various issues that were intended to be dealt with at the March CMC including: Reduction of Sample Vehicles, Sample Vehicle Testing, Disclosure and Confidentiality &amp; Information Sharing. I have set out a summary of each of these below:Reduction of Sample VehiclesThe parties in advance of the CMC had agreed that the Ford Litigation would benefit in reducing its cohort of vehicles. Having considered this carefully with counsel and with our experts to ensure that we were ensuring that, even with a reduction of vehicles, the PDD Trial would remain as widely binding and dispositive of the issues as possible, we agreed with the Defendants to reduce the number of Sample Vehicles to be tried at the trial from 12 to six. We remain confident that this decision does not impact the prospects of success, and indeed it may actually enhance them as it has meant the parties have been able to perform a sufficient level of testing on the vehicles to best present our case to the Court at the PDD Trial.&nbsp;Sample Vehicle TestingThis was a particularly thorny issue between the parties with agreement being found late in the day on the terms of the order (a copy of which is attached). In essence the Court ordered the parties to urgently being to jointly test the vehicles at various test houses across the UK. The Claimants had been pushing for this for a number of months and it was a relief when we finally had this enshrined in an order. A further update on the testing we have been undertaking is contained below.DisclosureThis was the biggest issue between the parties in March. The Defendants, understandably if we were wearing their shoes, did not want to actively participate in the disclosure exercise. They argued this was on grounds of proportionality and how much time and money it would cost for them to find documents, however the closer reality is that no Defendant actively wishes to be forced to disclose documents that may be damaging to their case. The parties, with active intervention from the Court, were able to reach a resolution and were able to agree on certain categories of documents that needed to be disclosed and a very tight timeframe for the disclosure of those documents, much to the dismay of the Defendants. The Ford Order is attached.&nbsp;We have already been taking receipt of substantial documents and reviewing them and the results are thus far, very promising. A further update on disclosure is below.&nbsp;Confidentiality &amp; Information sharingAs you will know, much of the information provided thus far in the Ford Litigation has been into a confidentiality ring, meaning limited people can see it. The same applies to the other Lead Cases of Mercedes, Renault/Nissan and Peugeot Citroën. The Claimants have been aggressive pursing a course of action, to uphold the principle of open justice, that the majority of documentation in this case should be open to the public and that there does not need to be such a strict and "}

